    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nLet's consider the following expression:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 5 + 3 \\times 7]\n\nContent:\nNotice that this expression contains addition and multiplication. So how do we compute its value?\n\nContent:\nTo help us, we have the following rule:\n\nContent:\n> When an expression contains only multiplication and addition (or subtraction), the multiplication should always be carried out first.\n\nContent:\nSo, we must first carry out the multiplication [MATH: (3\\times 7).] We can emphasize this using parentheses [MATH: (\\phantom{0}){:}]\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 5 + \\big({\\color{blue}3 \\times 7}\\big)]\n\nContent:\nHere, the parentheses tell us which part of the expression to do first.\n\nContent:\nNow, we evaluate our expression as follows:\n[MATH: 5 + (3 \u00d7 7)|=; 5 + 21|=; 26]\n\nContent:\nWatch out! If we carried out the operations in a different order, we'd obtain an incorrect answer!\n\nContent:\nFor example, if we perform the addition first, we get\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (5 + 3) \u00d7 7|=; 8 \u00d7 7|=; 56|\u00d7]\n\nContent:\nwhich is incorrect!\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions Where Multiplication Comes First\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 5 \\times 4 -7.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nMultiplication should be carried out before addition or subtraction. We can emphasize this by using parentheses:\n\n[MATH: 5 \\times 4 -7 = ({\\color{blue}5 \\times 4})-7]\n\nWe carry out the operations inside the parentheses first. We have\n\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}5 \\times 4} = {\\color{blue}20}.]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: (5 \u00d7 4) \u2212 7|=; 20 \u2212 7|=; 13|.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 4 \u00d7 7 + 3.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 84]\nb. [MATH: 82]\nc. [MATH: 24]\nd. [MATH: 34]\ne. [MATH: 31]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 4 \u00d7 7 \u2212 3 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 25]\nb. [MATH: 23]\nc. [MATH: 31]\nd. [MATH: 15]\ne. [MATH: 16]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions Where Multiplication Comes Second\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 4 + 10 \\times 2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nMultiplication should be carried out before addition or subtraction. We can emphasize this by using parentheses:\n\n[MATH: 4+ 10 \\times 2 = 4 + ({\\color{blue}10 \\times 2})]\n\nWe carry out the operations inside the parentheses first. We have\n\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}10 \\times 2} = {\\color{blue}20}.]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: 4 + (10 \u00d7 2)|=; 4 + 20|=; 24|.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 3 + 4 \u00d7 7.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 31]\nb. [MATH: 88]\nc. [MATH: 84]\nd. [MATH: 25]\ne. [MATH: 23]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 25 \u2212 4 \u00d7 3 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 15]\nb. [MATH: 60]\nc. [MATH: 66]\nd. [MATH: 63]\ne. [MATH: 13]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions Containing Two Products\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 3 \\times 9 - 5 \\times 5.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nMultiplication should be carried out before addition or subtraction. We can emphasize this by using parentheses:\n\n[MATH: 3 \\times 9 - 5 \\times 5 = ({\\color{blue}3 \\times 9}) - ({\\color{red}5 \\times 5})]\n\nNow, we evaluate the products:\n\n[MATH: 3 \u00d7 9|= 27; 5 \u00d7 5|= 25]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: (3 \u00d7 9) \u2212 (5 \u00d7 5)|=; 27 \u2212 25|=; 2|.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 3 \u00d7 4 + 2 \u00d7 3.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 42]\nb. [MATH: 30]\nc. [MATH: 54]\nd. [MATH: 18]\ne. [MATH: 12]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3 \u00d7 4 \u2212 2 \u00d7 3 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6]\nb. [MATH: 18]\nc. [MATH: 30]\nd. [MATH: 12]\ne. [MATH: 24]"
